     5	# Bootstrap secret inputs
     6	
     7	`[bootstrap.secrets]` declares the sensitive inputs a bootstrap configuration
     8	needs without storing their values in mise configuration. Values come from the
     9	environment, making secret managers such as [fnox](https://fnox.jdx.dev/) the
    10	provider boundary rather than adding provider-specific credentials to mise.
    11	
    12	```toml
    13	[bootstrap.secrets]
    14	cache_token = "MISE_CACHE_TOKEN"
    15	database_password = REDACTED
    16	  env = "PRODUCTION_DATABASE_PASSWORD",
    17	  description = "Production database password",
    18	}
    19	
    20	[bootstrap.files."/etc/example/service.env"]
    21	content = '''
    22	CACHE_TOKEN={{ secret(name="cache_token") }}
    23	DATABASE_PASSWORD=REDACTED secret(name="database_password") }}
    24	'''
    25	template = true
    26	owner = "root"
    27	group = "root"
    28	mode = "0600"
    29	```
    30	
    31	The short declaration maps a logical name directly to an environment variable.
    32	The table form also accepts `description` and `allow_empty = true`; empty values
    33	are rejected by default. mise resolves only the inputs referenced by selected
    34	file templates; an unused declaration does not block unrelated files. Referenced
    35	inputs are resolved and every template is rendered before any full-bootstrap
    36	mutation starts, so a missing input cannot leave a partially rendered file or
    37	allow earlier bootstrap steps to run.
    38	
    39	The `.env` example assumes values that can be written as single-line assignments.
    40	The `secret()` function inserts the value; it does not quote or escape it for
    41	shell, JSON, TOML, or another target format. Render and encode values according
    42	to the format consumed by the service, especially for quotes or newlines.
    43	
    44	## Supply inputs and check availability
    45	
    46	Use fnox to inject provider-backed values into the bootstrap process:
    47	
    48	```sh
    49	fnox exec -- mise bootstrap --yes
    50	fnox exec -- mise bootstrap plan
    51	```
    52	
    53	This is intentionally a loose integration. The machine running mise does not
    54	need fnox when its environment has already been populated, and mise does not
    55	know whether a value came from fnox, a CI secret, `systemd`, or a shell.
    56	
    57	For an attended one-off run, `--prompt-secrets` securely prompts for missing
    58	values. Prompted values remain in memory and are not exported:
    59	
    60	```sh
    61	mise bootstrap --prompt-secrets --yes
    62	mise bootstrap files apply --prompt-secrets
    63	mise bootstrap plan --prompt-secrets
    64	```
    65	
    66	`mise bootstrap secrets status` reports logical names, environment variable
    67	names, and `available`, `missing`, `empty`, or `invalid_unicode`; it never prints
    68	values. Add `--json` for machine-readable output or `--missing` to exit 1 when
   285	### Templates
   286	
   287	```toml
   288	[dotfiles]
   289	"~/.gitconfig" = { source = "dotfiles/gitconfig.tera", mode = "template" }
   290	```
   291	
   292	Templates can use `env`, `vars`, `exec()`, and the rest of the
   293	[template context](/templates.html). Applying a template writes its rendered
   294	content and gives the target the source file's permissions. A later apply
   295	also repairs changed permissions.
   296	
   297	`status`, `diff`, and `apply` render templates to check their output. This
   298	executes any `exec()` calls in those templates, using your trusted config.
   299	With `--dry-run`, mise skips rendering dotfile templates and labels them
   300	`(if changed)`. Other configuration expressions can still run during a dry
   301	run, so use it with trusted configuration.
